{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791369386,"nonce":"jz/sUX/UxSqGu8LCIWhivN7eIdg=","title":"HIR: retain scalar loads across disjoint byte-range stores","message":"Follow-up to 02563f8f234c45ce68f81f5479e139cb89be51fa68b0d9d7d74c3b01378d324f. The block-local load CSE pass keeps loads across stores only when their roots have different exact struct types. It discards a field load across a store to provably non-overlapping bytes of the same SSA pointer, leaving repeated loads for Cranelift to execute.\n\nImplement a bounded, memoized byte-address analysis for constant byte GEPs and scalar pointer/array strides. Do not merge different pointer roots, infer identities of loaded pointers, or infer struct layout. Keep calls, atomics, vector writes, volatile accesses, unknown offsets and overlapping writes conservative. Preserve load type identity. Verify same-object fields, partial overlap, aliases, pointer reloads, negative/wrapping offsets and cross-tier execution, then compare LLVM-off NUC benchmarks.\n\nThe originally proposed blanket Cranelift region annotations need further proof: in the pinned revision a store with no region fences every region, but a load with no region tracks only last_fence, not every tagged store. Unknown/raw reads cannot simply remain unclassified while potentially aliasing typed stores acquire separate regions. The runtime also emits stack-copy and dispatch-cell accesses beyond HIR Load/Store. A complete region partition is a separate follow-up; this change applies the proven disjointness in HIR instead.","files":null}]}